From: Richard Osterloh <richard.osterloh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] zephyr: tcp: time out reads with poll(), not SO_RCVTIMEO

zenoh-pico 1.10 runs the read and lease jobs on one executor thread
(zp_start_lease_task is a no-op). _z_tcp_zephyr_open sets SO_RCVTIMEO, but
Zephyr rejects it unless CONFIG_NET_CONTEXT_RCVTIMEO is set, and the failure
is only logged. recv() then blocks until data arrives, the lease job never
runs, no keep-alive is sent while the peer is quiet, and the router expires
the session after one lease.

Poll for Z_CONFIG_SOCKET_TIMEOUT before recv() and return SIZE_MAX on
timeout, which the client read job already treats as "nothing to read".

CONFIG_NET_CONTEXT_RCVTIMEO would do the same, but on an ESP32
(general_driver) enabling it stops the board in early boot before WiFi init.
---
--- a/src/link/transport/tcp/tcp_zephyr.c
+++ b/src/link/transport/tcp/tcp_zephyr.c
@@ -193,6 +193,14 @@
 }
 
 static size_t _z_tcp_zephyr_read(_z_sys_net_socket_t sock, uint8_t *ptr, size_t len) {
+    // SO_RCVTIMEO needs CONFIG_NET_CONTEXT_RCVTIMEO, and without it recv() blocks
+    // the single executor thread, so the lease job never sends a keep-alive.
+    // Time out here instead; the read job treats SIZE_MAX as "nothing to read".
+    // ponytail: uses Z_CONFIG_SOCKET_TIMEOUT, so a locator `tout=` is ignored.
+    struct zsock_pollfd pfd = {.fd = sock._fd, .events = ZSOCK_POLLIN};
+    if (zsock_poll(&pfd, 1, Z_CONFIG_SOCKET_TIMEOUT) <= 0) {
+        return SIZE_MAX;
+    }
     ssize_t rb = recv(sock._fd, ptr, len, 0);
     if (rb < (ssize_t)0) {
         return SIZE_MAX;
